# Templates

**The idea: write the class once with a placeholder type `T`. The compiler makes a version for each type you use.**

Like a cookie cutter where you choose the dough: chocolate, sugar, gingerbread. Same shape.

```cpp
template <typename T>
class myArray {
    T* array;
    int allocated_size;
};

myArray<int> beds(50);
myArray<std::string> names(50);
```

![the compiler makes myArray of int and myArray of string from one template](courses/diagrams/f6a9f3d3-3525-5a97-b7ae-c7c0c87daada/pic.svg)

`myArray<int>` and `myArray<string>` are **two separate classes**.

This is **static polymorphism** (decided at compile time): fast, but the type must be known when compiling, and each type adds more code.

**Check:** templates are which kind of polymorphism?

