# Queue Wrap-Around

**The idea: both indexes only move forward, and `% N` wraps them around.**

```cpp
// enqueue
Q[r] = e;
r = (r + 1) % N;

// dequeue
e = Q[f];
f = (f + 1) % N;
```

![f and r wrapping past the end of an eight-slot array](courses/diagrams/6d778345-a364-54ad-a4cd-00c0d70cc28d/steps.svg)

In step 3, the queue reads A B C D even though D is in slot 0. **Order starts at f**, not at index 0.

Dequeue doesn't erase. It just moves `f` past the slot.

> [!tip]
> On paper: draw N boxes, track f and r in two columns. Move exactly one per operation.

**Check:** trace the N = 4 queue below.

