# std::map and std::unordered_map

**The idea: `std::map` is a balanced BST, so it keeps keys sorted. `std::unordered_map` is a hash table with no order.**

```cpp
std::map<string, int> ids;
ids["science building"] = 1;
ids["eng"] = 0;
ids["art"] = 8;
for (auto& p : ids) cout << p.first << " ";   // art eng science building
```

Iterating a map = in-order walk = sorted.

**`std::map`:** usually a red-black tree. **O(log n) worst case.** Costs: one heap node per element, poor cache locality. (`set`, `multimap` are similar.)

**`std::unordered_map`:** hash table with chaining. O(1) average, **no order**. Can't list alphabetically.

**Check:** which keeps keys sorted?

